 Map - do not maintain insertion order, only one key can be null
 
  Map<Integer,String>map = new HashMap<>();
	   map.put(1, "efg");
	   map.put(2, "abc");
	   map.put(3,"bcd");
	   
	   for(int i: map.keySet()){
	       System.out.println(map.get(i));
	   }
	   
	   // can iterate like this as well to access key, value, can also set it
	   for(Map.Entry<Integer,String>mp : map.entrySet()){
	       System.out.println(mp.getKey() + mp.getValue());
	   }
 
 Internal working- First, the key is passed to hash function which generate a unique hashcode, which determines where the key-value pair should be stored. (index = hashcode % n) where n is arraysize
 
since different keys can generate same index(called collision), HashMap then uses Linked list for that(Balanced binary or Red Black tree after Java 8).
So if multiple pairs occur, they are stored in linkedlist inside bucket

takes O(1) time in put(), get() operations (assuming no collisions)



